              "blocking": 0,
              "total_blocking": 0
            },
            "body": "In route rules, a `**` in a `redirect` or `proxy` target is only filled in when the rule key ends in a plain `/**`. A named catch-all (`/**:path`) gets no `base`, so `to` is resolved inconsistently: a trailing `/**` appends the whole request path, and a `**` anywhere else is left literal.\n\nRequest `/old/a/b` (h3 2.0.3, same code on main in `src/rules/normalize.ts`):\n\n| Rule key | `redirect` | Location |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `/old/**` | `/new/**` | `/new/a/b` |\n| `/old/**:path` | `/new/**` | `/new/old/a/b` |\n| `/old/**` | `/new?from=**` | `/new?from=a/b` |\n| `/old/**:path` | `/new?from=**` | `/new?from=**` |\n\nTreating a trailing `/**:name` like `/**` when setting `base` for `redirect` and `proxy` would fix it. The current output doesn't match the docs either way, so this reads as a bug fix rather than a breaking change. Nitro's platform presets already emit `/new/a/b` for this rule (nitrojs/nitro#4707).\n\n_Reported with AI assistance._\n",
